  "content": {
    "problemStatement": "If you wanna party, if you, if you wanna party \n\nThen put your hands up\n\nChef wants to host a party with a total of NNN people.\n\nHowever, the party hall has a capacity of XXX people. Find whether Chef can host the party.\n\n\nInput Format\n\nThe first line of input will contain a single integer TTT, denoting the number of test cases.\nEach test case consists of two space-separated integers NNN and XXX  — the total number of people and the capacity of the party hall.\n\n\nOutput Format\nFor each test case, output on a new line, `YES`, if Chef can host the party and `NO` otherwise.\n\n\nEach character of the output may be printed in either uppercase or lowercase. That is, the strings `NO`, `no`, `nO`, and `No` will be treated as equivalent.",
    "constraints": [
      "1≤T≤1001 \\leq T \\leq 1001≤T≤100",
      "1≤N,X≤101 \\leq N, X \\leq 101≤N,X≤10"
    ],
    "inputFormat": "",
    "outputFormat": "",
    "notes": [],
    "sampleTestCases": [
      {
        "id": "sample_1",
        "type": "reg",
        "input": "4\n2 5\n4 3\n6 6\n10 9",
        "output": "YES\nNO\nYES\nNO",
        "explanation": "Explanation:\nTest case 111: Chef wants to host a party with 222 people. Since the capacity of the hall is 555, he can host the party.\nTest case 222: Chef wants to host a party with 444 people. Since the capacity of the hall is 333, he can not host the party.\nTest case 333: Chef wants to host a party with 666 people. Since the capacity of the hall is 666, he can host the party.\nTest case 444: Chef wants to host a party with 101010 people. Since the capacity of the hall is 999, he can not host the party."
      }
    ]
  },
